WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.000
-Our next guest
is a Grammy and Academy

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.934
Award-winning producer,
songwriter, and deejay.

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:06.400
The second season
of his podcast,

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.200
"The FADER Uncovered,"
is available now.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.968
Please welcome to the show
Mark Ronson, everybody.

00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:12.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:19.200
♪♪

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:25.767
♪♪

00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:27.200
Welcome to the show.
How are you?

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.133
-I'm great.
-It -- It's lovely to see you.

00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:32.267
And I want to start
by congratulating you.

00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:33.801
Recently married. There you go.
-Yes.

00:00:33.801 --> 00:00:35.534
-There's you and your wife,
Grace Gummer.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:37.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:40.167
-Yeah, it's -- it's --
It was wonderful.

00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:42.834
She's the best ever.
I don't -- I can't say enough.

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:44.300
And it was --
it was a wonderful day.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:45.801
-It was a great wedding.

00:00:45.801 --> 00:00:48.133
And I would imagine a little bit
scaled back because we are,

00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:50.000
of course, in the middle
of a pandemic, but --

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.434
-Yes.
-I'm curious.

00:00:51.434 --> 00:00:53.801
With someone like you who is
a famous music producer

00:00:53.801 --> 00:00:56.801
and deejay, do you have a band,
do you have a deejay?

00:00:56.801 --> 00:00:59.267
Or is it just too much pressure
to put on someone else?

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:01.834
-Well, I --
Weirdly, like, I hate deejays.

00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:03.367
It's so strange
because I am a deejay,

00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:05.133
and I was thinking like,
"Who would I have?"

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:06.767
And then I thought,
"Well, it's not fair,

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:08.400
that just 'cause
I don't like deejays..."

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.968
Everyone else
should be able to dance.

00:01:09.968 --> 00:01:12.968
But a bunch of the guys
in the band

00:01:12.968 --> 00:01:14.601
that were going to play
are some of my friends

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:16.934
who play in the Roots
and play on "Jimmy Fallon."

00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:18.601
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-And -- Yes.

00:01:18.601 --> 00:01:20.100
-Yeah, it's very --
-Amazing, too.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.133
-Very nice pool
for a wedding band.

00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:25.167
-They are great, and they were
also playing earlier that week.

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:27.934
Remember -- Barack Obama
was having his huge 60th.

00:01:27.934 --> 00:01:29.968
-Yes, I do believe there
was some press about that.

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:31.534
-There was some press
about that. Of course.

00:01:31.534 --> 00:01:33.100
So -- So -- And, you know,

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.934
as we're scaling back
our wedding

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:37.033
because it's COVID
and we're kind of looking at --

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:39.234
I mean, I can't believe
I'm, like, judging my wedding

00:01:39.234 --> 00:01:40.534
against, like, Obama's 60th.

00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:42.701
Remember like, through the week,
it was like --

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:44.601
well, now they've disinvited
200 people

00:01:44.601 --> 00:01:45.834
and now it's only gonna be 50.

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:48.734
And so we had --
we disinvited everybody

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:50.934
at our wedding except,
you know, immediate family.

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:53.901
So when the guys got there
from the Roots band,

00:01:53.901 --> 00:01:55.300
I kind of wanted to be like,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.734
"So what was the Obama --
it was really small, right?"

00:01:57.734 --> 00:01:59.434
They were like "No.
That's -- It was wild.

00:01:59.434 --> 00:02:01.033
It was, like, crazy."

00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:03.467
And I was just like -- Like,
they hadn't slept in three days.

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:05.234
They've probably
been jamming with, like,

00:02:05.234 --> 00:02:07.100
Quincy Jones and Dave Chappelle.
Like, "Whatever."

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.300
And I'm like, "Okay, well,
just play the jazz tunes."

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.634
Like -- Like, "I'm sorry.
This isn't going to be that."

00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:16.300
I think it was a good, like,
hungover Sunday for them.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.934
-Very nice. Good -- Good vibe.

00:02:18.934 --> 00:02:22.501
-You have -- You have a podcast
where you interview musicians.

00:02:22.501 --> 00:02:24.534
You have a documentary series
on Apple TV

00:02:24.534 --> 00:02:25.968
called "Watch the Sound."

00:02:25.968 --> 00:02:28.234
The same sort of idea.
-Yes.

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:29.734
-When you were talking
to musicians --

00:02:29.734 --> 00:02:31.434
And obviously
it's what you do for a living.

00:02:31.434 --> 00:02:33.601
But a lot of these people,
you were listening

00:02:33.601 --> 00:02:35.601
to their music
before it was your living.

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:37.000
-Yes.
-How do you separate

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.400
those moments
of being someone who is,

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.968
you know,
in their field, a peer,

00:02:40.968 --> 00:02:43.133
but also a giant fan
of their work?

00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:45.200
-I mean, it's impossible.
I just, like, sit on my hands.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.167
And I'm like,
"Don't be an idiot."

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:48.601
No. I mean, it's true.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:51.567
I make music, and it's nice
that some of these people

00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:53.300
think of me as a peer, like --

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.767
But I'm talking to Big Boi
from "Outkast."

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:59.601
My favorite concert ever
was Outkast in 1999 in Miami.

00:02:59.601 --> 00:03:02.167
And just, like, these are people
that have soundtracked my life.

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:03.701
So I always try
and wait the moment.

00:03:03.701 --> 00:03:05.133
Because you don't want
to start up

00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:06.634
and you're like,
"Der, I love you."

00:03:06.634 --> 00:03:09.067
'Cause then it just seems like
you're trying to win them over.

00:03:09.067 --> 00:03:11.100
I don't know. But --
And then, occasionally,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.334
I'll interview some rapper
who's, like,

00:03:13.334 --> 00:03:16.033
a young up-and-coming person
like Rico Nasty.

00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:17.968
And at the very end,
she'll be like,

00:03:17.968 --> 00:03:19.200
"So, like, what do you do?

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.901
You're like a journalist
or something?"

00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:22.834
Like, she has -- she has no idea
I make music,

00:03:22.834 --> 00:03:24.200
which is also great.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.567
So I kind of -- I --
I don't mind either way.

00:03:26.567 --> 00:03:28.901
-I would imagine, like,
interviewing someone

00:03:28.901 --> 00:03:32.200
is one thing, but you also
collaborate with them.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.267
And that, it must be even
more important not to

00:03:35.267 --> 00:03:38.000
sort of geek out or --
or be a fanboy,

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.067
because then they might not
take you seriously.

00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:41.968
-Yeah. I mean, I think that --

00:03:41.968 --> 00:03:43.734
I mean, the ultimate example,
of course,

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:45.801
of working with, like,
somebody who is, like,

00:03:45.801 --> 00:03:47.701
basically a god is, like,
Paul McCartney.

00:03:47.701 --> 00:03:51.501
And I feel like on day one,
you're allowed to be an idiot.

00:03:51.501 --> 00:03:53.801
And like -- I've been producing
records for like 15 years.

00:03:53.801 --> 00:03:55.834
It was like everything
was Kryptonite in my hands.

00:03:55.834 --> 00:03:57.367
Like, it's Paul McCartney,
you know?

00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:59.133
-Yeah.
-So then I think he gives you

00:03:59.133 --> 00:04:01.501
a day to be like that
because he's used to --

00:04:01.501 --> 00:04:03.100
He has that effect on everyone.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.033
But then, like, day two, like,

00:04:05.033 --> 00:04:07.133
"You better come
with the heat, bro,

00:04:07.133 --> 00:04:09.801
or Sir Paul is gonna fire you."

00:04:09.801 --> 00:04:11.100
So -- So, yeah.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.534
So it's just about
getting over those things.

00:04:12.534 --> 00:04:14.400
But, like, Q-Tip,
Ghostface Killah,

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.701
all these people I grew up with
who were like --

00:04:16.701 --> 00:04:18.367
grew up listening
to their music,

00:04:18.367 --> 00:04:19.834
when you're finally
in the studio with them,

00:04:19.834 --> 00:04:21.667
it is kind of surreal, you know?

00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:24.200
-I can only imagine.
You -- You've worked

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.868
with people like Amy Winehouse
and Lady Gaga and Bruno Mars.

00:04:26.868 --> 00:04:28.767
And I'm just wondering,
over the course of this career

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:30.234
where you've worked with this,
you know,

00:04:30.234 --> 00:04:34.000
diverse slate
of singers and musicians,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.567
are there moments where you hear
a lyric for the first time

00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:38.801
or you hear, you know,
a note for the first time

00:04:38.801 --> 00:04:41.267
that, even having worked
in this for so long,

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:43.634
that you're just taken aback
and in awe?

00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:46.267
-Yeah, like,
I definitely remember

00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:49.567
when we were writing "Shallow"
and you're futzing around

00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:51.701
and everybody's
got an instrument

00:04:51.701 --> 00:04:53.234
and going through chords and --

00:04:53.234 --> 00:04:54.667
And Lady Gaga,
she's playing this piano.

00:04:54.667 --> 00:04:56.734
And the moment --
She's looking for words.

00:04:56.734 --> 00:04:58.868
She's saying phrases.
And then she says --

00:04:58.868 --> 00:05:00.701
She sang,
"Tell me something, boy,"

00:05:00.701 --> 00:05:02.000
or "Tell me something, girl.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.434
Are you happy
in this modern world?"

00:05:03.434 --> 00:05:05.901
And, like, the way she sang it
and her voice,

00:05:05.901 --> 00:05:07.567
like, your hairs just stand up.

00:05:07.567 --> 00:05:09.000
You're like, "Okay.
What's the next line?"

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.133
Then you --
Then you get this excitement.

00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:12.834
But, yeah. No.

00:05:12.834 --> 00:05:15.734
It's -- It's amazing
when those moments happen.

00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:19.067
-I feel like, thanks to a number
of music documentaries

00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:21.067
over the last,
you know, decade-plus,

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:24.367
we have a greater appreciation
for what a music producer does.

00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:27.367
And you just realize
there's such a delicate

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:30.267
fiddling and changing and --
you know, deciding, you know,

00:05:30.267 --> 00:05:32.234
what to bring up
and what to bring down.

00:05:32.234 --> 00:05:35.167
Do you ever hear
one of your songs years later

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:38.734
and regret choices
you made or...?

00:05:38.734 --> 00:05:40.367
-Yeah.

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:42.467
Like, I listen to all of them,
and I'm just like --

00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:44.534
I don't listen to my own music,
like, active--

00:05:44.534 --> 00:05:46.534
That's not true. I just...
-[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.534 --> 00:05:48.934
No -- No, I -- I don't.
I don't make it a regular thing.

00:05:48.934 --> 00:05:51.367
But, occasionally, like,
you hear a song and it's, like,

00:05:51.367 --> 00:05:53.434
something you did 15 years ago,
and you're like,

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:55.501
"Oh, I get it. I was, like,
this aggressive kid.

00:05:55.501 --> 00:05:57.400
I was trying to throw
the whole kitchen sink.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.067
Now I know why I should have,
like, chilled out."

00:06:00.067 --> 00:06:04.033
But, occasionally, like --
I was in the airport, actually.

00:06:04.033 --> 00:06:07.834
And I heard this song playing,
like, far -- from the speaker.

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:09.200
Like, some duty-free.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.567
I was in the whiskey store
at 7:00 in the morning.

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:12.934
No. Heathrow. Sorry.

00:06:12.934 --> 00:06:14.734
And I was like,
"Oh, that's cool."

00:06:14.734 --> 00:06:16.667
I was like,
"Those drums sound cool."

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:18.968
Because I always hear drums, and
if the drums have a good sound,

00:06:18.968 --> 00:06:20.234
I, like,
want to hear the song.

00:06:20.234 --> 00:06:22.601
And I ended up Shazaming it,
and I looked,

00:06:22.601 --> 00:06:24.334
and I was like,
"Oh, I made that."

00:06:24.334 --> 00:06:25.567
Like, I --

00:06:25.567 --> 00:06:26.901
[ Laughter ]

00:06:26.901 --> 00:06:29.033
It wasn't so amazing, but I was
like, "That's so funny."

00:06:29.033 --> 00:06:31.133
I was like, "I wonder who did
the drums on this song."

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:32.901
And I forgot
that I had done that song.

00:06:32.901 --> 00:06:34.567
-I really -- I mean, that's --
that's really funny.

00:06:34.567 --> 00:06:36.200
Like, "This is really great.
I got to --

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:37.834
Oh. Oh, well. Look at that.

00:06:37.834 --> 00:06:39.534
[ Laughter ]

00:06:39.534 --> 00:06:41.100
-Yeah.
One bottle of Jack Daniels.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.367
Thank you.

00:06:42.367 --> 00:06:44.334
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:44.334 --> 00:06:46.701
Congrats on the podcast.
Can't wait to see the show.

00:06:46.701 --> 00:06:47.834
-Thank you.

00:06:47.834 --> 00:06:49.734
-Mark Ronson.
"The FADER Uncovered"

00:06:49.734 --> 00:06:51.901
is available wherever
you get your podcasts.

00:06:51.901 --> 00:06:54.467
And "Watch the Sound"
is streaming on Apple TV+.

